const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How long does it take to sell a house in the Axarquía?',
    a: 'It varies a lot. A well-priced apartment on the coast sometimes sells within a few months. A rural finca is often on the market for a year or more, especially if there are questions about its legal status. Allow another one to three months between the reservation contract (arras) and the notary.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I renovate my house before selling?',
    a: 'Usually not completely. What pays off is fixing visible defects (damp, leaks, cracks, broken systems) and a fresh look: paint, garden and pool in order. You rarely get the cost of a brand new kitchen or bathroom back. The page on repairs before selling explains what does and does not pay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I sell a house with AFO status?',
    a: 'Yes. A house with an AFO declaration (DAFO) from the town hall can be sold and registered in the land registry. Without that declaration selling is harder: many buyers and banks walk away. Start the application well before you sell, as it often takes months.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who pays the plusvalía tax when selling?',
    a: 'The municipal plusvalía (tax on the increase in land value) is in principle paid by the seller. Since November 2021 you pay nothing if you can show there was no increase in value, and you can choose the cheaper of two calculation methods.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I do not live in Spain. Why does the buyer hold back 3%?',
    a: 'If the seller is not tax resident in Spain, the buyer must withhold 3% of the price and pay it to the Spanish tax office as an advance on your tax on the gain. You then file your own return. If your tax was lower than the 3%, you can claim the difference back.',
  },
];
